<endoscopic_lifting.at
<endoscopy_xi.at

{ ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  epstar_xi_compare.at

  Compare the two independent endoscopic lifts available on this branch:

     epstar_std (endoscopic_lifting.at)   vs   lift_std_xi (endoscopy_xi.at)

  across a whole block on the endoscopic group H.  They should agree.  They
  do not, everywhere: see OPEN ISSUE below.

  Both take a standard module on H and return [ParamPol] on real forms of G.
  They report "no lift" differently -- epstar_std returns an empty LIST,
  lift_std_xi returns a list holding a zero ParamPol -- so those two cases are
  counted separately rather than lumped together.

  USAGE
     atlas> <epstar_xi_compare.at
     atlas> compare(SO(4,5),[0,0,1,1]/2,SO(4,5),SO(5,4),[0,0,0,0],144)
     atlas> compare(Sp(4,R),[1/2,1/2],Sp(4,R),Sp(4,R),[0,0],6)

     compare(G0,s,G_rf,G_ic,xi,n):
        (H,M,)=endoscopic_group(G0,s), B=block_of(H.trivial), and for i<n
        compare  epstar_std(B[i],G_ic,M)  with  lift_std_xi(B[i],G_rf,xi).
        G_rf and G_ic are the same group, named as RealForm / InnerClass.

  OPEN ISSUE (7/31/26).  The two do not agree everywhere.

     compare(SO(4,5),[0,0,1,1]/2,SO(4,5),SO(5,4),[0,0,0,0],144)
        agree 90 | differ 32 | only epstar 6 | only xi 12 | both none 4

     compare(Sp(4,R),[1/2,1/2],Sp(4,R),Sp(4,R),[0,0],6)
        agree 4 | differ 2 | only epstar 0 | only xi 0 | both none 0

  Two different-looking phenomena, and they may or may not have one cause:

  (a) PAIRING.  The Sp(4,R) pair is a straight transposition -- block elements
      4 and 5 get each other's answer:
         i=4  epstar {x10}          xi {x8}{x7}
         i=5  epstar {x8}{x7}       xi {x10}
      Same multiset, attached to swapped block elements.  That looks like the
      two implementations pair x_H with x_G by different conventions, not like
      either lift being wrong.  Several of the SO(4,5) "differ" rows have the
      same transposed shape (e.g. 21/22, 31/32).

  (b) NOT ONLY PAIRING.  Over the SO(4,5) block the multisets of lifts do NOT
      match: 128 nonempty from epstar_std against 134 from lift_std_xi, and
      the two disagree about which elements lift at all (6 where only
      epstar_std gives something, 12 where only lift_std_xi does).  So a
      pairing convention cannot be the whole story there.

  Whether one of the two is wrong, or the two maps genuinely differ, is
  unresolved.  Note that the four values quoted in N. Arancibia's report --
  Sp(4,R) B[0] and B[1], SO(4,5) H.trivial, and SO(4,5) B[4] -- all agree
  between the two, so the reference values alone do not expose any of this;
  it takes the block sweep.

  History: before the fixes to *(mat,KGBElt_gen) in synthetic2.at and to the
  double make_dominant on x_G in epstar, this comparison could not be run at
  all -- epstar_std threw "(y,gamma) is not valid" partway through the block,
  and lift_std_xi threw "Torus factor does not define a valid strong
  involution" on the same elements.  Both errors came from the synthetic2.at
  formula, so note that those fixes moved lift_std_xi's output too, not only
  epstar_std's; comparisons against pre-fix numbers must account for that.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------ }

{ one-line serialization of a lift, so results can be compared and sorted as
  plain text; to_string@ParamPol is multi-line and not usable for that }
set exc_ser([ParamPol] L)=string:
  if #L=0 then "NOLIFT"
  else let s="" in
    for c@p in L[0] do
      s:=s+"{"+to_string(c)+"*x"+to_string(#p.x)+
          ",l"+to_string(p.lambda)+",n"+to_string(p.nu)+"}"
    od;
    if s="" then "ZERO" else s fi
  fi

set exc_epstar(Param p_H,InnerClass G_ic,mat M)=string:
  exc_ser(epstar_std(p_H,G_ic,M))

set exc_xi(Param p_H,RealForm G_rf,ratvec xi)=string:
  exc_ser(lift_std_xi(p_H,G_rf,xi))

{ full comparison; prints a line for each disagreement, then the totals }
set compare(RealForm G0,ratvec s,RealForm G_rf,InnerClass G_ic,ratvec xi,int n)=void:
  let (H,M,)=endoscopic_group(G0,s) then
  B=block_of(H.trivial) then
  m=min(n,#B) then
  agree=0, differ=0, only_ep=0, only_xi=0, neither=0 in
  prints("H = ",H,"   block size ",#B,"; comparing 0..",m-1);
  for i:m do
    let e=exc_epstar(B[i],G_ic,M) then x=exc_xi(B[i],G_rf,xi) in
    if e="NOLIFT" and x="ZERO" then neither+:=1
    elif e="NOLIFT" then only_xi+:=1; prints(" ",i," only xi     : ",x)
    elif x="ZERO" then only_ep+:=1; prints(" ",i," only epstar : ",e)
    elif e=x then agree+:=1
    else differ+:=1;
      prints(" ",i," DIFFER",new_line,"      epstar_std : ",e,
                                      new_line,"      lift_std_xi: ",x)
    fi
  od;
  prints("agree ",agree," | differ ",differ," | only epstar ",only_ep,
         " | only xi ",only_xi," | both none ",neither)

{ the indices where the two disagree }
set differing(RealForm G0,ratvec s,RealForm G_rf,InnerClass G_ic,ratvec xi,int n)=[int]:
  let (H,M,)=endoscopic_group(G0,s) then B=block_of(H.trivial) then m=min(n,#B) in
  ##for i:m do
      let e=exc_epstar(B[i],G_ic,M) then x=exc_xi(B[i],G_rf,xi) in
      if e!=x and not (e="NOLIFT" and x="ZERO") then [i] else [] fi
    od

{ the SO(4,5) case from N. Arancibia's report }
set exc_demo()=void:
  compare(SO(4,5),[0,0,1,1]/2,SO(4,5),SO(5,4),[0,0,0,0],144)
